    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The music industry creates content for people all over the world. Yet, while a diverse audience may listen to music, the executive teams at music labels often lack diversity. This paper addresses diversity gaps in label leadership through the lens of gender. I argue that having a more balanced representation of gender could result in more innovative teams and inclusive company cultures. I support this argument through an analysis of the responses that traditional labels had towards the digital music revolution, in comparison with the response of Atlantic Records, which is co-led by a woman (Julie Greenwald). This paper includes an examination of available literature, a case study on Greenwald and her work at Atlantic Records, and insight from five interviews of female leaders in the industry. I observe that female leaders often manifest innovation and inclusivity and are more likely to encourage transformational leadership. I also find the support of male mentors and allies to be crucial for the trajectory of women in the music industry.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
